# Forbidden patterns for Java files (*.java). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: java
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB - patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation.
# Patterns are POSIX ERE, matched case-sensitively with `grep -anE`.
# Bans console debug output (keeps logger.info/warn/error intact) plus a block
# of JVM RCE/TLS sinks below.
# printStackTrace ban is no-arg only; printStackTrace(writer) is allowed (logging idiom).
# Copy to your project as `.forbidden-patterns/java.txt`.

(^|[^A-Za-z_])System\.out\.print(ln)?[[:space:]]*\(	Use a logger (slf4j/Log4j), not System.out.print/println
(^|[^A-Za-z_])System\.err\.print(ln|f)?[[:space:]]*\(	Use logger.error(), not System.err.print/println/printf
\.printStackTrace[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*\)	Log the exception via the logger, not e.printStackTrace(); pass a PrintWriter to capture it instead

# --- Security: JVM RCE / reflection / TLS sinks ---
# Java ships no linter template, no hook lint and a placeholder CI job, so this
# file is the only gate in front of these lines. readObject is matched in its
# NO-ARG call form only, so a class's own `private void readObject(ObjectInputStream in)`
# hook is not flagged.
# checkServerTrusted is matched ONLY with an EMPTY BODY on the same line - the
# `{}` / `{ }` of the copy-pasted trust-all TrustManager. A real implementation
# (a pinning or delegating one, whose body is on the following lines) does not
# fire, and neither does a call to `delegate.checkServerTrusted(...)`. The limit
# is the line-at-a-time scan: an empty body split across two lines is not seen.
# setAccessible(true) is NOT an active rule - see the opt-in block at the bottom.
(^|[^A-Za-z_])new[[:space:]]+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\.)*ObjectInputStream[[:space:]]*\(	Java deserialization of untrusted bytes is RCE (CVE-2015-4852 class) - use JSON/protobuf with a schema
\.readObject[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*\)	ObjectInputStream.readObject() on untrusted input is RCE - deserialize a schema-checked format instead
(^|[^A-Za-z_])Runtime\.getRuntime[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*\)[[:space:]]*\.exec	Runtime.exec() is a command-injection sink - use ProcessBuilder with a fixed argv
(^|[^A-Za-z_])checkServerTrusted[[:space:]]*\([^)]*\)[^{]*\{[[:space:]]*\}	An EMPTY checkServerTrusted() accepts every certificate - that is TLS validation switched off (MITM risk); validate the chain or keep the platform TrustManager
ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER	ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER disables TLS hostname checking (MITM risk) - keep the default verifier

# Optional (opt-in) — uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(TODO|FIXME|XXX)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	TODO/FIXME/XXX not allowed - open a ticket instead
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])System\.out\.printf[[:space:]]*\(	Use a logger, not System.out.printf (FP-prone in CLI tools)
# setAccessible(true) ships OFF: it is a design smell, not a sink, and it is
# ordinary in Jackson/Spring/Hibernate glue, JUnit ReflectionTestUtils-style test
# helpers and any DI container - an always-on rule blocks correct commits in
# every one of those. Enable it in an application module that should contain no
# reflection of its own.
# \.setAccessible[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*true[[:space:]]*\)	setAccessible(true) defeats access control and final-field safety - drop the reflection (off by default: routine in framework glue and tests)
